 method used to evaluate claims about population parameters based on sample data. It involves formulating null and alternative hypotheses, selecting a significance level, calculating a test statistic, and making decisions based on p-values. The process is critical for validating statistical models and ensuring that conclusions drawn from data are supported by evidence. Key components of hypothesis testing include the formulation of hypotheses, significance levels, test statistics, and the interpretation of results. The assumptions of independence, normality, and homogeneity of variance are vital for the validity of hypothesis tests, and violations can lead to misleading conclusions [Data: Entities (37, 621); Relationships (136, 550)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of the F-test in hypothesis testing\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The F-test is a specific method used within hypothesis testing to determine the significance of regression coefficients and to compare variances among groups. It is particularly important in the context of ANOVA, where it assesses whether there are significant differences between group means. The F-statistic is calculated as the ratio of the variance between group means to the variance within groups, and a significant F-statistic indicates that at least one group mean is different from the others. The F-test relies on assumptions of normality, independence, and homogeneity of variances, and violations can lead to incorrect conclusions [Data: Entities (958); Relationships (1070, 1086)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Importance of regression coefficients\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Regression coefficients are essential parameters in regression models that quantify the relationship between independent and dependent variables. They indicate the expected change in the dependent variable for a one-unit change in the independent variable, holding other variables constant. Understanding regression coefficients is crucial for making predictions and informed decisions based on model findings. However, issues such as multicollinearity can complicate the interpretation of these coefficients, leading to inflated standard errors and unreliable estimates. Proper assessment of variables and their relationships is vital for robust regression analysis [Data: Entities (378); Relationships (260, 2190)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The significance of p-values in hypothesis testing\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The p-value is a critical measure in hypothesis testing that quantifies the probability of observing the data under the null hypothesis. It helps researchers determine whether to reject the null hypothesis based on a pre-defined significance level. A low p-value indicates strong evidence against the null hypothesis, while a high p-value suggests insufficient evidence to reject it. Understanding the role of p-values is essential for interpreting statistical results, but it is important to note that p-values do not measure the size or importance of an effect. The assumptions of random sampling, independence, normality, and homogeneity of variance must be met for valid p-value interpretation [Data: Entities (620); Relationships (618)].\"\n        }\n    ]\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}], "created": 1771167762, "model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_f97eff32c5", "usage": {"completion_tokens": 914, "prompt_tokens": 11225, "total_tokens": 12139, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 1024}}, "prompt_filter_results": [{"prompt_index": 0, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "jailbreak": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}]}, "input": {"messages": [{"content": "\nYou are an AI assistant that helps a human analyst to perform general information discovery. Information discovery is the process of identifying and assessing relevant information associated with certain entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n621,ANOVA TABLE,\"**ANOVA Table**\n\nThe ANOVA (Analysis of Variance) table is a fundamental statistical tool employed to analyze the differences among group means within a sample. It is particularly useful in the context of regression analysis and hypothesis testing, where it helps to determine whether there are statistically significant differences between the means of different groups.\n\nThe primary purpose of the ANOVA table is to summarize the sources of variation in a dataset. It partitions the total variation observed in the data into components attributable to different sources, typically including the variation due to the treatment (or group) effects and the variation due to random error. This partitioning allows researchers to assess the significance of the group differences.\n\nThe ANOVA table typically includes the following components:\n\n1. **Source of Variation**: This section categorizes the sources of variation, which usually include \"\"Between Groups\"\" (variation due to the treatment or group differences) and \"\"Within Groups\"\" (variation due to random error).\n\n2. **Sum of Squares (SS)**: This measures the total variation in the data. It is divided into:\n   - **SS Between**: The sum of squares due to the differences between group means.\n   - **SS Within**: The sum of squares due to the differences within each group.\n\n3. **Degrees of Freedom (df)**: This indicates the number of independent values that can vary in the analysis. For the ANOVA table:\n   - **df Between**: Calculated as the number of groups minus one (k - 1).\n   - **df Within**: Calculated as the total number of observations minus the number of groups (N - k).\n\n4. **Mean Squares (MS)**: This is calculated by dividing the sum of squares by the corresponding degrees of freedom:\n   - **MS Between** = SS Between / df Between\n   - **MS Within** = SS Within / df Within\n\n5. **F-statistic**: This is the ratio of the mean square between groups to the mean square within groups:\n   \\[\n   F = \\frac{MS \\text{ Between}}{MS \\text{ Within}}\n   \\]\n   The F-statistic is used to test the null hypothesis that all group means are equal.\n\n6. **P-value**: This indicates the probability of observing the data, or something more extreme, under the null hypothesis. A low p-value (typically less than 0.05) suggests that there is significant evidence to reject the null hypothesis, indicating that at least one group mean is different from the others.\n\nThe assumptions underlying the ANOVA table include:\n- **Independence**: The observations must be independent of each other.\n- **Normality**: The data in each group should be approximately normally distributed.\n- **Homogeneity of Variances**: The variances among the groups should be approximately equal.\n\nThese assumptions are crucial because violations can lead to incorrect conclusions. For instance, if the assumption of normality is violated, the F-test may not be valid, potentially leading to Type I or Type II errors. Similarly, if the variances are not equal, the results of the ANOVA may be misleading.\n\nIn summary, the ANOVA table is an essential tool in statistical analysis, particularly in regression contexts, for assessing the significance of differences among group means. By summarizing the sources of variation and providing a framework for hypothesis testing, it enables researchers to draw meaningful conclusions from their data.\",9\r\n37,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,\"**Hypothesis Testing**\n\nHypothesis testing is a fundamental statistical method employed in econometrics and other fields to make inferences about population parameters based on sample data. It serves as a systematic approach to evaluate the validity of a hypothesis concerning a population parameter, often in the context of regression analysis. The primary objective of hypothesis testing is to determine whether the data at hand provide sufficient evidence to support or reject a specific claim, known as the null hypothesis (H₀), in favor of an alternative hypothesis (H₁).\n\nThe process of hypothesis testing typically involves several key components:\n\n1. **Formulation of Hypotheses**: The first step is to clearly define the null hypothesis (H₀) and the alternative hypothesis (H₁). The null hypothesis generally posits that there is no effect or no difference, while the alternative hypothesis suggests the presence of an effect or a difference.\n\n2. **Selection of Significance Level (α)**: A significance level, often set at 0.05 or 0.01, is chosen to determine the threshold for rejecting the null hypothesis. This level indicates the probability of committing a Type I error, which occurs when the null hypothesis is incorrectly rejected.\n\n3. **Test Statistic Calculation**: A test statistic is computed from the sample data, which quantifies the degree of deviation from the null hypothesis. Common test statistics include the t-statistic for t-tests and the z-statistic for z-tests, depending on the sample size and whether the population variance is known.\n\n4. **Decision Rule**: Based on the calculated test statistic and the chosen significance level, a decision rule is established. If the test statistic falls into the critical region defined by the significance level, the null hypothesis is rejected; otherwise, it is not rejected.\n\n5. **Interpretation of Results**: The outcome of the hypothesis test is interpreted in the context of the research question. A rejection of the null hypothesis suggests that there is sufficient evidence to support the alternative hypothesis, while a failure to reject the null hypothesis indicates insufficient evidence to make such a claim.\n\nHypothesis testing is particularly important in econometrics as it allows researchers to assess the significance of regression coefficients, thereby validating the relationships posited by econometric models. It provides a framework for making informed decisions based on empirical data analysis, which is crucial for drawing conclusions about economic phenomena.\n\n**Assumptions of Hypothesis Testing**: \n\nThe validity of hypothesis testing relies on several key assumptions, including:\n\n- **Independence**: Observations in the sample must be independent of one another. Violation of this assumption can lead to biased estimates and incorrect conclusions.\n- **Normality**: For many tests, particularly those involving the t-statistic, the sampling distribution of the test statistic should be approximately normally distributed, especially as sample sizes increase due to the Central Limit Theorem.\n- **Homogeneity of Variance**: The variance among groups being compared should be roughly equal. This assumption is critical for tests like ANOVA.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to incorrect inferences, such as Type I or Type II errors, where researchers either reject a true null hypothesis or fail to reject a false null hypothesis, respectively.\n\nIn summary, hypothesis testing is a vital statistical method that enables researchers to make informed decisions about population parameters based on sample data. By systematically evaluating hypotheses, it plays a crucial role in validating econometric models and drawing conclusions about economic relationships.\",48\r\n958,F-TEST,\"The F-test is a statistical test widely utilized in econometrics and statistics to compare variances and assess the overall significance of regression models. It serves multiple purposes, particularly in the context of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) and regression analysis.\n\n### Definition and Purpose\nThe F-test is primarily designed to evaluate whether there are significant differences between the means of two or more groups. In regression analysis, it is used to determine if the model as a whole is statistically significant, meaning that at least one of the independent variables has a non-zero coefficient. This is crucial for validating the effectiveness of the regression model in explaining the variability of the dependent variable.\n\n### Formula\nThe F-statistic is calculated using the following formula:\n\n\\[\nF = \\frac{\\text{MS}_{\\text{between}}}{\\text{MS}_{\\text{within}}}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(\\text{MS}_{\\text{between}}\\) is the mean square between the groups, which measures the variance among the group means.\n- \\(\\text{MS}_{\\text{within}}\\) is the mean square within the groups, which measures the variance within each group.\n\n### Assumptions\nThe F-test relies on several key assumptions:\n1. **Normality**: The data in each group should be approximately normally distributed. This is particularly important when sample sizes are small.\n2. **Independence**: Observations must be independent of one another. This means that the data collected from one group should not influence the data from another group.\n3. **Homogeneity of Variances**: The variances among the groups should be equal (homoscedasticity). This assumption can be tested using Levene's test or Bartlett's test.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to incorrect conclusions. For instance, if the assumption of homogeneity of variances is violated, the F-test may produce misleading results, potentially leading to Type I or Type II errors.\n\n### Applications\nThe F-test is commonly applied in various contexts:\n- **ANOVA**: It is used to compare the means of three or more groups to determine if at least one group mean is different from the others.\n- **Regression Analysis**: In multiple regression, the F-test assesses the overall significance of the model, helping to determine if the independent variables collectively explain a significant portion of the variance in the dependent variable.\n\n### Interpretation\nThe outcome of the F-test is interpreted through the F-statistic and its corresponding p-value. A high F-statistic value, along with a low p-value (typically less than 0.05), indicates that there is a statistically significant difference between the group means or that the regression model is significant. Conversely, a low F-statistic and a high p-value suggest that the null hypothesis (which states that there are no differences among the group means or that the model is not significant) cannot be rejected.\n\nIn summary, the F-test is a fundamental statistical tool in econometrics, essential for comparing variances and assessing the significance of regression models. Its proper application hinges on meeting the underlying assumptions, and its results provide critical insights into the relationships among variables in various analytical contexts.\",9\r\n2679,Z-TEST,\"The Z-test is a statistical test employed in econometrics to assess whether there is a significant difference between the means of two groups. It is particularly useful when the sample size is large (typically n > 30) and the population variance is known or can be accurately estimated. The Z-test is grounded in the principles of inferential statistics, allowing researchers to make conclusions about population parameters based on sample statistics.\n\n### Purpose\nThe primary purpose of the Z-test is to evaluate hypotheses regarding the means of two groups. It is commonly used in various fields, including economics, psychology, and social sciences, to compare the effectiveness of different treatments, interventions, or conditions.\n\n### Formula\nThe formula for the Z-test statistic is given by:\n\n\\[\nZ = \\frac{\\bar{X_1} - \\bar{X_2}}{\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1} + \\frac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( \\bar{X_1} \\) and \\( \\bar{X_2} \\) are the sample means of the two groups,\n- \\( \\sigma_1^2 \\) and \\( \\sigma_2^2 \\) are the population variances,\n- \\( n_1 \\) and \\( n_2 \\) are the sample sizes of the two groups.\n\n### Assumptions\nThe Z-test relies on several key assumptions:\n1. **Normality**: The data in each group should be approximately normally distributed, especially important when sample sizes are small. However, with larger samples, the Central Limit Theorem suggests that the sampling distribution of the mean will be approximately normal regardless of the shape of the population distribution.\n2. **Independence**: The observations within each group must be independent of each other. This means that the selection of one observation does not influence the selection of another.\n3. **Known Variance**: The population variances of the two groups should be known or can be reliably estimated. If the variances are unknown, alternative tests such as the t-test may be more appropriate.\n\n### Importance\nThe Z-test is significant in hypothesis testing as it provides a method for determining whether observed differences between group means are statistically significant or could have occurred by random chance. This is crucial for making informed decisions based on empirical data.\n\n### Interpretation\nUpon calculating the Z-test statistic, it is compared against critical values from the standard normal distribution to determine the p-value. A low p-value (typically less than 0.05) indicates that the null hypothesis (which posits no difference between the group means) can be rejected, suggesting that there is a statistically significant difference between the means of the two groups.\n\n### Consequences of Violation\nIf the assumptions of the Z-test are violated, the results may be misleading. For instance, if the normality assumption is not met and the sample size is small, the Z-test may yield inaccurate results. Similarly, if the independence assumption is violated, the test may produce inflated Type I error rates, leading to incorrect conclusions about the significance of the differences observed.\n\nIn summary, the Z-test is a fundamental statistical tool in econometrics for comparing means between two groups, provided that its assumptions are satisfied. Its application allows researchers to draw meaningful inferences from sample data, contributing to the broader understanding of economic and social phenomena.\",6\r\n575,ALTERNATIVE HYPOTHESIS,\"The Alternative Hypothesis is a fundamental concept in statistical hypothesis testing, serving as a critical counterpart to the Null Hypothesis. It is defined as a statement that contradicts the Null Hypothesis, which typically posits that there is no effect or no difference in the population being studied. The Alternative Hypothesis, therefore, asserts that there is a statistically significant effect or relationship present, indicating that the observed data is not due to random chance alone.\n\nMathematically, the Alternative Hypothesis is often denoted as \\( H_a \\) or \\( H_1 \\). It can take various forms depending on the nature of the research question and the statistical test being employed. For instance, in a two-tailed test, the Alternative Hypothesis might state that a parameter (such as a mean or proportion) is not equal to a specified value, while in a one-tailed test, it may specify that the parameter is either greater than or less than that value. \n\nThe importance of the Alternative Hypothesis lies in its role in guiding the research process. It provides a framework for researchers to formulate their expectations about the data and to design experiments or studies that can test these expectations. By establishing an Alternative Hypothesis, researchers can focus their analysis on detecting effects or differences that are of substantive interest, thereby contributing to the advancement of knowledge in their field.\n\nIn the context of hypothesis testing, the Alternative Hypothesis is evaluated against the Null Hypothesis through statistical tests. If the evidence from the data is strong enough to reject the Null Hypothesis, researchers may conclude that the Alternative Hypothesis is supported. This decision is typically made based on a p-value, which quantifies the probability of observing the data, or something more extreme, assuming the Null Hypothesis is true. A low p-value (commonly below a threshold of 0.05) suggests that the observed effect is statistically significant, leading to the rejection of the Null Hypothesis in favor of the Alternative Hypothesis.\n\nThe formulation of the Alternative Hypothesis also has implications for the assumptions underlying statistical tests. For instance, the validity of the conclusions drawn from hypothesis testing relies on certain assumptions, such as the independence of observations, normality of the sampling distribution, and homogeneity of variances. Violating these assumptions can lead to incorrect conclusions regarding the Alternative Hypothesis, potentially resulting in Type I errors (incorrectly rejecting the Null Hypothesis when it is true) or Type II errors (failing to reject the Null Hypothesis when the Alternative Hypothesis is true).\n\nIn summary, the Alternative Hypothesis is a critical component of hypothesis testing that posits the existence of an effect or difference, opposing the Null Hypothesis. It is essential for guiding research inquiries, formulating statistical tests, and interpreting results. Understanding the Alternative Hypothesis, along with its relationship to the Null Hypothesis and the assumptions of statistical tests, is vital for conducting rigorous and meaningful empirical research.\",4\r\n589,SIGNIFICANCE TEST,A significance test is a statistical procedure that determines whether the observed data is consistent with a specified hypothesis.,3\r\n378,REGRESSION COEFFICIENTS,\"**Regression Coefficients**\n\nRegression coefficients are fundamental parameters in a regression model that quantify the relationship between independent variables and a dependent variable. They serve as the backbone of regression analysis, allowing researchers and analysts to understand how changes in independent variables affect the dependent variable.\n\nIn a typical regression equation, the relationship can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + ... + \\beta_nX_n + \\epsilon \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y \\) is the dependent variable,\n- \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept,\n- \\( \\beta_1, \\beta_2, ..., \\beta_n \\) are the regression coefficients corresponding to independent variables \\( X_1, X_2, ..., X_n \\),\n- \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\nThe regression coefficients (\\( \\beta \\)) indicate the expected change in the dependent variable \\( Y \\) for a one-unit change in the respective independent variable \\( X_i \\), holding all other variables constant. For instance, if \\( \\beta_1 = 2 \\), it implies that an increase of one unit in \\( X_1 \\) is associated with an increase of two units in \\( Y \\), assuming other variables remain unchanged.\n\nThe importance of regression coefficients lies in their ability to provide insights into the strength and direction of relationships within the data. Positive coefficients suggest a direct relationship, while negative coefficients indicate an inverse relationship. The magnitude of the coefficients reflects the size of the effect that the independent variables have on the dependent variable.\n\nHowever, the interpretation of regression coefficients can be complicated by issues such as multicollinearity, which occurs when independent variables are highly correlated with each other. This can lead to inflated standard errors and unreliable coefficient estimates, making it difficult to ascertain the true effect of each independent variable on the dependent variable.\n\nIn summary, regression coefficients are essential for understanding the dynamics between independent and dependent variables in a regression model. They provide a quantitative measure of relationships, enabling analysts to make informed predictions and decisions based on the model's findings.\",19\r\n592,EVIDENCE,,1\r\n645,NORMALITY TEST,A normality test is a statistical test used to determine if a data set is well-modeled by a normal distribution.,1\r\n954,CHI-SQUARED DISTRIBUTION,The Chi-Squared Distribution is used to test hypotheses about the variance in regression models.,1\r\n2678,POWER FUNCTION,The power function is a statistical concept that describes the probability of correctly rejecting the null hypothesis when it is false.,1\r\n2680,DISTRIBUTION OF X,\"The distribution of X refers to the probability distribution of a random variable X, which is analyzed in the context of hypothesis testing.\",1\r\n3433,\"N(0,1) DISTRIBUTION\",\"The N(0,1) distribution refers to the standard normal distribution, which is a probability distribution with a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1.\",1\r\n3434,TEST STATISTIC,\"**Test Statistic**\n\nA test statistic is a standardized value derived from sample data that plays a crucial role in hypothesis testing. It serves as a numerical summary of the data that allows researchers to make inferences about the population from which the sample is drawn. The primary purpose of a test statistic is to determine whether to reject the null hypothesis (H0) in favor of an alternative hypothesis (H1).\n\nThe calculation of a test statistic typically involves the following general formula:\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{\\hat{\\theta} - \\theta_0}{SE(\\hat{\\theta})}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\hat{\\theta} \\) is the estimated parameter from the sample, \\( \\theta_0 \\) is the hypothesized value under the null hypothesis, and \\( SE(\\hat{\\theta}) \\) is the standard error of the estimate. The specific form of the test statistic can vary depending on the type of hypothesis test being conducted, such as t-tests, z-tests, chi-square tests, or F-tests.\n\nThe importance of the test statistic lies in its ability to provide a basis for decision-making regarding the null hypothesis. By comparing the calculated test statistic to a critical value from a relevant statistical distribution (e.g., normal, t, chi-square), researchers can assess the likelihood of observing the sample data under the null hypothesis. If the test statistic falls into the critical region, the null hypothesis is rejected, indicating that the sample provides sufficient evidence to support the alternative hypothesis.\n\nThe interpretation of the test statistic is contingent upon the context of the hypothesis test. A larger absolute value of the test statistic typically indicates a greater deviation from the null hypothesis, suggesting stronger evidence against it. Conversely, a test statistic close to zero implies that the sample data is consistent with the null hypothesis.\n\nIn summary, the test statistic is a fundamental component of hypothesis testing, providing a standardized measure to evaluate the evidence against the null hypothesis based on sample data. Its calculation, interpretation, and application are essential for drawing valid conclusions in statistical analysis.\",1\r\n379,VARIABLES,\"**Variables in Econometrics**\n\nIn econometrics, variables are fundamental elements or factors that can change and are utilized in statistical models to analyze relationships among different economic phenomena. They serve as the building blocks of econometric analysis, allowing researchers to quantify and interpret the dynamics between various economic indicators.\n\nVariables can be categorized into two main types: dependent and independent variables. The dependent variable is the outcome or response that the model aims to explain or predict, while independent variables are the predictors or factors that influence the dependent variable. For instance, in a model analyzing the relationship between income and savings, income would typically be considered an independent variable, while savings would be the dependent variable.\n\nThe importance of variables in econometric models cannot be overstated. They are essential for establishing relationships and testing hypotheses about economic behavior. By manipulating and analyzing these variables, econometricians can derive insights into how changes in one variable may affect another, thereby informing policy decisions and economic forecasts.\n\nHowever, the presence of multicollinearity among independent variables can significantly impact the estimates produced by a regression model. Multicollinearity occurs when two or more independent variables are highly correlated, leading to difficulties in estimating the individual effect of each variable on the dependent variable. This can result in inflated standard errors, making it challenging to determine the statistical significance of the predictors. Consequently, it is crucial for econometricians to assess the correlation among independent variables and address multicollinearity issues to ensure the reliability of their model estimates.\n\nIn summary, variables in econometrics are measurable traits or characteristics that are analyzed within a model. They are pivotal in understanding economic relationships and are subject to various influences, including multicollinearity, which can affect the accuracy of model estimates. Proper identification and treatment of variables are essential for robust econometric analysis and interpretation.\",6\r\n574,NULL HYPOTHESIS,\"The Null Hypothesis is a fundamental concept in statistical hypothesis testing, serving as a general statement or default position that posits there is no relationship between two measured phenomena or no effect or difference between groups. It is a specific type of statistical hypothesis that provides a baseline for comparison against an alternative hypothesis, which posits that there is an effect or a difference.\n\nMathematically, the Null Hypothesis is often denoted as \\( H_0 \\). For example, in the context of comparing two means, the Null Hypothesis might be stated as \\( H_0: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2 \\), indicating that the means of two populations are equal. In contrast, the alternative hypothesis, denoted as \\( H_a \\) or \\( H_1 \\), would assert that \\( H_a: \\mu_1 \\neq \\mu_2 \\), suggesting that there is a significant difference between the two means.\n\nThe importance of the Null Hypothesis lies in its role as a starting point for statistical testing. It allows researchers to use statistical methods to determine whether there is enough evidence to reject it in favor of the alternative hypothesis. The process of hypothesis testing typically involves calculating a test statistic and comparing it to a critical value or using a p-value to assess the strength of the evidence against the Null Hypothesis.\n\nKey assumptions underlying the Null Hypothesis include:\n1. **Independence**: The observations should be independent of one another.\n2. **Normality**: The data should be approximately normally distributed, especially in small samples.\n3. **Homogeneity of Variance**: The variances among groups being compared should be equal.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to incorrect conclusions. For instance, if the independence assumption is violated, the test may yield inflated Type I error rates, leading to false rejections of the Null Hypothesis. Similarly, if the normality assumption is not met, particularly in small samples, the validity of the test results may be compromised, necessitating the use of non-parametric tests or transformations.\n\nIn practice, the Null Hypothesis is tested using various statistical methods, including t-tests, ANOVA, and regression analysis, depending on the nature of the data and the research question. The outcome of the hypothesis test will either lead to the rejection of the Null Hypothesis, suggesting that there is sufficient evidence to support the alternative hypothesis, or the failure to reject it, indicating that there is not enough evidence to conclude a significant effect or difference exists.\n\nIn summary, the Null Hypothesis is a critical component of statistical analysis, providing a framework for testing relationships and differences between variables. Its formulation and testing are essential for drawing valid conclusions in empirical research, making it a cornerstone of inferential statistics.\",21\r\n129,CONFIDENCE INTERVAL,\"**Confidence Interval**\n\nA Confidence Interval (CI) is a statistical concept that provides a range of values derived from sample statistics, which is likely to contain the true value of an unknown population parameter. This range is crucial in statistical analysis, particularly in the context of regression analysis and hypothesis testing, as it quantifies the uncertainty associated with the estimation of population parameters.\n\nThe CI is typically expressed with a specified level of confidence, commonly 95%, indicating that if the same sampling procedure were repeated numerous times, approximately 95% of the calculated intervals would contain the true population parameter. Mathematically, a confidence interval for a population mean can be represented as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{CI} = \\bar{x} \\pm z \\left( \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right)\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(\\bar{x}\\) is the sample mean,\n- \\(z\\) is the z-score corresponding to the desired confidence level,\n- \\(s\\) is the sample standard deviation, and\n- \\(n\\) is the sample size.\n\nThe importance of confidence intervals lies in their ability to provide a measure of uncertainty around an estimate. They allow researchers to make inferences about population parameters based on sample data, facilitating decision-making in various fields, including economics, medicine, and social sciences.\n\nConfidence intervals are constructed under certain assumptions, which include:\n1. **Random Sampling**: The sample must be randomly selected from the population to ensure that it is representative.\n2. **Normality**: For large sample sizes (typically \\(n \\geq 30\\)), the Central Limit Theorem suggests that the sampling distribution of the sample mean will be approximately normally distributed, regardless of the population distribution. For smaller samples, the population should ideally be normally distributed.\n3. **Independence**: The observations in the sample must be independent of each other.\n\nThe consequences of violating these assumptions can lead to inaccurate confidence intervals. For instance, if the sample is not random, the CI may not accurately reflect the population parameter. Similarly, if the normality assumption is violated in small samples, the CI may be too narrow or too wide, leading to misleading conclusions.\n\nIn regression analysis, confidence intervals are particularly useful for estimating the parameters of the model, such as coefficients. They provide insight into the precision of these estimates and help assess the significance of predictors. For example, if a confidence interval for a regression coefficient does not include zero, it suggests that the predictor is statistically significant at the given confidence level.\n\nMoreover, confidence intervals can be affected by multicollinearity, which occurs when independent variables in a regression model are highly correlated. This can inflate the standard errors of the coefficients, leading to wider confidence intervals and potentially obscuring the significance of predictors.\n\nIn summary, a confidence interval is a vital tool in statistical inference, providing a range of values that likely contains the true population parameter with a specified level of confidence. It is essential for understanding the uncertainty associated with estimates and plays a critical role in hypothesis testing and regression analysis.\",23\r\n220,MARGINAL PROPENSITY TO CONSUME,\"The Marginal Propensity to Consume (MPC) is a fundamental economic concept that quantifies the relationship between changes in disposable income and changes in consumption expenditure. Specifically, the MPC measures the proportion of additional income that a household or consumer chooses to spend on consumption rather than save. This relationship is crucial for understanding consumer behavior and the overall dynamics of the economy.\n\nMathematically, the MPC can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\nMPC = \\frac{\\Delta C}{\\Delta Y}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\Delta C\\) represents the change in consumption and \\(\\Delta Y\\) denotes the change in disposable income. This formula illustrates that the MPC is the ratio of the change in consumption to the change in income, highlighting how much of each additional dollar of income is spent on consumption.\n\nThe importance of the MPC lies in its implications for economic policy and forecasting. A higher MPC indicates that consumers are likely to spend a larger portion of any additional income, which can stimulate economic growth through increased demand for goods and services. Conversely, a lower MPC suggests that consumers are more inclined to save additional income, which may dampen economic activity. Understanding the MPC is essential for policymakers, particularly in the context of fiscal stimulus measures, as it helps predict the effectiveness of tax cuts or direct payments in boosting consumption and, consequently, economic growth.\n\nThe MPC is also closely related to other economic concepts, such as the Average Propensity to Consume (APC), which measures the total consumption as a proportion of total income. While the MPC focuses on changes in income and consumption, the APC provides a broader view of consumption behavior over a range of income levels.\n\nIn terms of assumptions, the MPC operates under the premise that consumers behave rationally and respond predictably to changes in income. It assumes that consumers have stable preferences and that their consumption decisions are influenced primarily by their current income levels. However, violations of these assumptions can lead to discrepancies in the predicted versus actual consumption behavior. For instance, during periods of economic uncertainty, consumers may choose to save more despite an increase in income, resulting in a lower-than-expected MPC.\n\nIn summary, the Marginal Propensity to Consume is a critical concept in economics that measures the change in consumption resulting from a change in disposable income. It is defined as the proportion of additional income that consumers spend on consumption, and it plays a vital role in understanding consumer behavior and informing economic policy. The MPC is calculated using the formula \\(MPC = \\frac{\\Delta C}{\\Delta Y}\\) and is influenced by various factors, including consumer confidence and economic conditions. Understanding the MPC helps economists and policymakers gauge the potential impact of income changes on overall economic activity.\",8\r\n124,F DISTRIBUTION,\"The F Distribution is a crucial probability distribution in statistics, particularly prominent in the contexts of analysis of variance (ANOVA) and regression analysis. It plays a significant role in statistical inference, especially when assessing the significance of model parameters and comparing variances across different groups.\n\n### Definition and Importance\nThe F Distribution is defined as the ratio of two independent chi-squared variables divided by their respective degrees of freedom. Specifically, if \\( X \\) and \\( Y \\) are independent chi-squared random variables with \\( d_1 \\) and \\( d_2 \\) degrees of freedom, respectively, then the random variable \\( F \\) defined as:\n\n\\[\nF = \\frac{X/d_1}{Y/d_2}\n\\]\n\nfollows an F distribution with \\( d_1 \\) and \\( d_2 \\) degrees of freedom. This distribution is non-negative and skewed to the right, making it particularly useful for hypothesis testing in various statistical applications.\n\n### Applications\nThe F Distribution is primarily utilized in the following contexts:\n\n1. **Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)**: In ANOVA, the F statistic is used to determine whether there are significant differences between the means of three or more groups. The null hypothesis typically states that all group means are equal, while the alternative hypothesis posits that at least one group mean is different. The F statistic is calculated as the ratio of the variance between the group means to the variance within the groups.\n\n2. **Regression Analysis**: In regression, the F Distribution is employed to test the overall significance of the regression model. The F statistic in this context compares the model with predictors to a model with no predictors (the intercept-only model). A significant F statistic indicates that at least one predictor variable has a non-zero coefficient.\n\n3. **Chow Test**: The F Distribution is also used in the Chow test, which assesses whether the coefficients in two different regression models are equal. This is particularly useful in structural break analysis.\n\n### Assumptions\nThe application of the F Distribution in hypothesis testing relies on several key assumptions:\n\n1. **Normality**: The populations from which the samples are drawn should be normally distributed. This assumption is particularly important for small sample sizes, as the F Distribution is sensitive to deviations from normality.\n\n2. **Independence**: The samples must be independent of each other. This means that the selection of one sample does not influence the selection of another.\n\n3. **Homogeneity of Variances**: The variances of the populations being compared should be equal (homoscedasticity). This assumption is critical for the validity of the F test, as violations can lead to incorrect conclusions.\n\n### Consequences of Violation\nViolating these assumptions can lead to misleading results. For instance, if the normality assumption is violated, particularly with small sample sizes, the F statistic may not follow the F distribution, leading to incorrect p-values. Similarly, if the independence assumption is violated, the test results may be biased. Lastly, if the homogeneity of variances assumption is not met, the F test may produce inflated Type I error rates, resulting in false positives.\n\nIn summary, the F Distribution is a fundamental tool in statistical analysis, particularly in ANOVA and regression contexts. Its ability to facilitate hypothesis testing regarding variances and model significance makes it indispensable in econometric analysis. Understanding its properties, applications, and underlying assumptions is essential for accurate statistical inference.\",10\r\n1058,LIKELIHOOD RATIO TEST,,5\r\n3690,MAXIMUM LIKELIHOOD PROCEDURE,\"The Maximum Likelihood procedure is a statistical method for estimating parameters of a model by maximizing the likelihood function, often used in the context of regression analysis.\",2\r\n620,P-VALUE,\"The p-value is a fundamental concept in statistical hypothesis testing, serving as a critical measure for determining the significance of results. It quantifies the probability of observing the data, or something more extreme, under the assumption that the null hypothesis is true. Specifically, the p-value is defined as the probability of obtaining a test statistic at least as extreme as the one observed, given that the null hypothesis holds.\n\nIn the context of hypothesis testing, the p-value plays a crucial role in decision-making. Researchers typically set a significance level, denoted as α (alpha), which is the threshold for determining whether to reject the null hypothesis. Commonly used values for α are 0.05, 0.01, and 0.10. If the p-value is less than or equal to α, the null hypothesis is rejected, suggesting that the observed effect is statistically significant. Conversely, if the p-value is greater than α, there is insufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis, indicating that the results may be due to random chance.\n\nThe interpretation of the p-value is essential for understanding the results of hypothesis tests. A smaller p-value indicates stronger evidence against the null hypothesis, while a larger p-value suggests weaker evidence. For instance, a p-value of 0.03 implies that there is a 3% probability of observing the data, or something more extreme, if the null hypothesis is true. This level of significance would typically lead researchers to reject the null hypothesis in favor of the alternative hypothesis.\n\nThe importance of the p-value extends beyond mere decision-making; it also provides insight into the strength of the evidence. However, it is crucial to note that the p-value does not measure the size or importance of an effect. A statistically significant result (low p-value) does not necessarily imply that the effect is practically significant or meaningful in a real-world context.\n\nThe p-value is subject to certain assumptions that must be met for its interpretation to be valid. These assumptions include the following:\n\n1. **Random Sampling**: The data should be collected through a process of random sampling to ensure that the sample is representative of the population.\n2. **Independence**: Observations should be independent of one another, meaning that the outcome of one observation does not influence another.\n3. **Normality**: For certain tests (e.g., t-tests), the data should be approximately normally distributed, especially in small samples.\n4. **Homogeneity of Variance**: In tests comparing groups (e.g., ANOVA), the variances among the groups should be roughly equal.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to misleading p-values and incorrect conclusions. For example, if the independence assumption is violated, the p-value may be artificially inflated or deflated, leading to incorrect inferences about the null hypothesis.\n\nIn summary, the p-value is a critical statistical measure in hypothesis testing that indicates the probability of observing the data under the null hypothesis. It aids researchers in making informed decisions about the significance of their results, provided that the underlying assumptions are satisfied. Understanding the p-value's role, interpretation, and limitations is essential for accurate statistical analysis and drawing valid conclusions from empirical research.\",3\r\n1626,DISTURBANCE VARIANCE,\"Disturbance variance refers to the variability in the error term of a model, which can affect the accuracy of estimates.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n580,ANOVA TABLE,GUJARATI,Gujarati authored the book that includes the ANOVA table as a key statistical tool for analysis,273\r\n136,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,FORECASTING,Hypothesis testing is often used in econometrics to validate forecasting models and their predictions.,57\r\n1070,F-TEST,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,The F-test is a method used in hypothesis testing to determine the significance of regression coefficients,57\r\n3511,Z-TEST,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,The Z-test is a specific method used within hypothesis testing to evaluate the means of different groups,54\r\n543,ALTERNATIVE HYPOTHESIS,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,\"**Alternative Hypothesis and Hypothesis Testing**\n\nHypothesis testing is a fundamental statistical method used to make inferences about population parameters based on sample data. It involves comparing two competing hypotheses: the null hypothesis (H₀) and the alternative hypothesis (H₁). The null hypothesis typically represents a statement of no effect or no difference, while the alternative hypothesis posits that there is an effect or a difference.\n\nThe alternative hypothesis is crucial in hypothesis testing as it provides a statement that researchers aim to support through their analysis. It is formulated to reflect the research question or the effect that the researcher is interested in detecting. For example, if a researcher is testing a new drug, the null hypothesis might state that the drug has no effect on patients (H₀: μ₁ = μ₂), while the alternative hypothesis would suggest that the drug does have an effect (H₁: μ₁ ≠ μ₂).\n\nThe process of hypothesis testing involves several key steps:\n\n1. **Formulation of Hypotheses**: The researcher clearly defines the null and alternative hypotheses.\n2. **Selection of Significance Level (α)**: A significance level is chosen, commonly set at 0.05, which indicates the probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when it is true (Type I error).\n3. **Data Collection**: Relevant data is collected through experiments or observational studies.\n4. **Test Statistic Calculation**: A test statistic is computed from the sample data, which measures how far the sample statistic deviates from the null hypothesis. Common test statistics include the t-statistic for t-tests and the z-statistic for z-tests.\n5. **Decision Rule**: Based on the test statistic and the chosen significance level, a decision is made to either reject or fail to reject the null hypothesis.\n6. **Conclusion**: The results are interpreted in the context of the research question, providing insights into the validity of the alternative hypothesis.\n\nThe interpretation of the results hinges on the comparison of the p-value (the probability of observing the test statistic under the null hypothesis) with the significance level. If the p-value is less than α, the null hypothesis is rejected in favor of the alternative hypothesis, suggesting that the observed effect is statistically significant.\n\nThe importance of the alternative hypothesis lies in its role in guiding research and providing a framework for statistical inference. It allows researchers to test specific theories and hypotheses, contributing to the advancement of knowledge in various fields.\n\nIn summary, hypothesis testing is a systematic method that compares the null hypothesis against the alternative hypothesis to assess statistical significance. The alternative hypothesis serves as a critical component of this process, representing the effect or difference that researchers seek to establish through empirical evidence.\",52\r\n544,SIGNIFICANCE TEST,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,Significance tests are used within the framework of hypothesis testing to evaluate hypotheses,51\r\n260,REGRESSION COEFFICIENTS,LINEAR REGRESSION MODEL,Regression coefficients are derived from the linear regression model and indicate the strength and direction of relationships between variables,49\r\n550,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,EVIDENCE,Evidence is used in hypothesis testing to determine whether to accept or reject the null hypothesis,49\r\n628,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,NORMALITY TEST,Normality tests are often conducted as part of hypothesis testing to ensure that the assumptions of the statistical methods are met,49\r\n1036,CHI-SQUARED DISTRIBUTION,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,The Chi-Squared Distribution is utilized in hypothesis testing to evaluate the variance in regression models.,49\r\n3508,POWER FUNCTION,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,The power function is used in hypothesis testing to determine the likelihood of correctly rejecting the null hypothesis,49\r\n3512,DISTRIBUTION OF X,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,The distribution of X is analyzed in hypothesis testing to understand the behavior of the test statistic,49\r\n4380,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,\"N(0,1) DISTRIBUTION\",\"The N(0,1) distribution is used in Hypothesis Testing to determine critical values\",49\r\n4382,HYPOTHESIS TESTING,TEST STATISTIC,\"The test statistic is a crucial component in hypothesis testing, used to determine whether to reject the null hypothesis\",49\r\n2190,VARIABLES,REGRESSION,\"Variables are essential components of regression analysis, as they represent the data being modeled\",45\r\n526,NULL HYPOTHESIS,CONFIDENCE INTERVAL,The confidence interval is used to determine whether to reject the null hypothesis based on the estimated values,44\r\n489,CONFIDENCE INTERVAL,PROBABILITY,Probability is used to determine the likelihood that a confidence interval contains the true parameter value,35\r\n524,MARGINAL PROPENSITY TO CONSUME,NULL HYPOTHESIS,The marginal propensity to consume is used to illustrate the null hypothesis in the context of the text,29\r\n1086,F DISTRIBUTION,ANOVA,The F distribution is used in the context of ANOVA to determine the significance of the results,27\r\n4658,LIKELIHOOD RATIO TEST,NULL HYPOTHESIS,The likelihood ratio test is used to evaluate the null hypothesis in statistical modeling,26\r\n4731,MAXIMUM LIKELIHOOD PROCEDURE,SHALABH,\"Shalabh describes the Maximum Likelihood procedure in his econometrics chapter, demonstrating his expertise in statistical estimation methods.\",26\r\n618,P-VALUE,NULL HYPOTHESIS,The p-value is used to determine whether to reject the null hypothesis in statistical tests,24\r\n2032,DISTURBANCE VARIANCE,CONFIDENCE INTERVAL,\"Disturbance variance affects the calculation of confidence intervals, impacting the reliability of statistical conclusions\",24\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\nOutput:", "role": "user"}], "parameters": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "frequency_penalty": 0.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1, "presence_penalty": 0.0, "temperature": 0.0, "top_p": 1.0, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}}}, "key": "chat_create_community_report_e03a0ec26cad3720c615536740d53f418022df9dcd76ec9a9e522ab3045133ad_v2"}